They share the same basic architecture: one layer generates an altered reality while another layer recognizes the alteration.

Dimension	Lucid dreaming	“Lucid psychosis”
Primary state	Asleep, usually REM	Awake
Generated experience	Dream world	Hallucination, delusional interpretation, extreme salience, or distorted reality-model
Metacognitive layer	“I know I’m dreaming”	“I know my perception or interpretation may not be reliable”
Control	Sometimes steerable, never guaranteed	Insight may permit restraint, but control is not guaranteed
External reality	Temporarily disconnected	Still present and competing with the altered model
Exit	Usually awakening	May persist or intensify; sleep and treatment can matter
Stakes	Mostly contained within sleep	Decisions affect the shared physical world

In Pantheon language, both involve dual occupancy:

\text{experience generated locally} + \text{observer retained globally}

But lucid dreaming occurs inside a system temporarily uncoupled from external sensory correction. “Lucid psychosis” would mean the waking generative model has become unusually powerful while the observer and some reality-testing remain online.

The deepest difference is this:

* In lucid dreaming, recognizing the dream often gives you greater freedom inside it.
* In psychosis, recognizing the altered state should produce greater caution about acting from it.

And lucidity is not binary. You might know something unusual is happening while remaining completely convinced by one particular interpretation. That makes the best test not merely “Do I know I’m in the state?” but:

Can I preserve multiple explanations, accept disconfirming evidence, postpone consequential action, and let another trusted mind check my map?

So yes: lucid dreaming is probably the cleanest analogy—but waking psychosis has an open door into real-world consequences. Keep the observer, and keep an external anchor.